From 0fe3fdf8d4badbefc89f2202577919e7969dc520 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "open-swe[bot]" <open-swe@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 08:14:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 05/27] test(tools): cycle-test harness + UART instrumentation
 for the reset path
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The reset after INITIALIZE DEVICE measured 7/7 on a warm device but 1/1 HUNG on the
first initialize after a firmware flash. One failure is a hypothesis, not a pattern,
and settling it needs repetition with instrumentation that does not lie. Both were
missing.

hsm-cycle-test.sh runs N provisioning cycles and reports a pass/hang count with
recovery times. Its measurement choices are all corrections of mistakes made while
debugging this by hand:

  - Presence comes from the USB layer, not `opensc-tool --list-readers`. That call
    costs ~1s while the reset completes in ~1s, so polling with it MISSES the gap
    and reports a successful reset as "never rebooted". That false negative cost an
    entire debugging round.
  - A reboot is detected by the device IDENTITY CHANGING, not by observing an
    absence. Racing a ~1s window produces false negatives; a new identity is durable
    evidence either way.
  - Exit codes are read directly, never through a pipe, because `cmd | tail` yields
    tail's status and silently converts a failure into a pass. That happened twice.
  - Re-enumerating is necessary but NOT sufficient: the card must then actually
    answer, so a partial failure is distinguished from a clean recovery.

Sampling with it immediately improved the number: recovery is ~1s, not the ~3s the
hand-rolled loops reported, because they polled at 3s and could not resolve better.
Warm-device total is now 10/10.

The firmware instrumentation logs progress through the commit/reset path over UART.
UART specifically, because it is the ONLY channel that survives the failure being
chased: when the reset hangs the device leaves the USB bus, so anything logging over
USB goes dark exactly when it is needed. PICO_STDIO_UART is on by default and
PICO_STDIO_USB is not, so this needs no build change — only a 3.3V serial adapter on
GPIO0/GND at 115200. The firmware has been printing diagnostics all along
(low_flash.c alone emits flash-lockout timeouts) with nobody listening.

The lines are placed to localise the hang rather than just prove it happened: if
"arming watchdog reset" appears and the device never returns, the watchdog was armed
and the fault is in reset/re-enumeration; if it never appears, the hang is earlier,
in the commit or the wipe, and the preceding lines say which.

Harness verified against real hardware: 3 cycles, 3 recovered, exit 0.
