MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An earlier round measured the reset after INITIALIZE DEVICE at 7/7 on a warm device
but 1/1 HUNG on the first initialize after a firmware flash, and that was nearly
written up as a genuine cold-boot defect with a documented workaround.

It was not a defect. The firmware had been built with -DPICO_BOARD=pico2 — RP2350A
with a 4MB flash map — on a Waveshare RP2350-PiZero, which is RP2350B with 16MB and
a different default UART (see waveshare_rp2350_pizero.h, present in the SDK the
whole time). Rebuilding against the correct board definition made the cold path pass
on the first attempt and on every attempt since: 9/9 recovering in 1-2s via
hsm-cycle-test.sh, cold path included.

The lesson, recorded because it cost three rounds of hardware debugging: a generic
PICO_BOARD target that BOOTS is not evidence that it is correct. The device
enumerated, answered APDUs, imported keys, signed, and verified — everything worked
well enough to hide the mismatch until one flash-heavy path started failing
intermittently. Verify what the hardware IS before reasoning about how it behaves.

Build this board with:
    cmake .. -DPICO_BOARD=waveshare_rp2350_pizero
