From 301557fa36e239af2fe01caab85eb2abeb8ea16b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "open-swe[bot]" <open-swe@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 09:33:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07/27] fix(hsm): size the commit timeout from the hardware,
 never reset on an unconfirmed commit
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two defects in the reset-after-INITIALIZE path, both mine.

1. The commit timeout was 10s. The data region is FLASH_SIZE/2, so on this 16MB
   board that is 8MB = ~2048 sectors of 4KB, and a sector erase costs ~30-45ms on
   RP2350 — a large wipe legitimately runs 60-90 SECONDS. 10s was not a tight bound,
   it was far below the NORMAL case, so the sync reported failure on erases that
   were proceeding perfectly. Raised to 180s. Overshooting costs nothing (the wait
   returns as soon as the queue drains) while undershooting hangs the device, so the
   consequences are asymmetric and the bound should be generous.

2. On an unconfirmed commit it fell back to an ASYNC commit and then reset ANYWAY —
   the worst ordering available, since "unconfirmed" most likely means the erase is
   still in flight and the reset lands inside it. Now it reports SW_EXEC_ERROR and
   stays alive. An operator who gets an error and retries is strictly better off than
   one holding a device that needs someone to walk into the datacenter.

MEASURED, and this is an improvement rather than a fix: full-chain cycles went from
2/3 to 14/15. The residual ~7% still hangs with the device leaving the USB bus, and
since the firmware now declines to reset whenever the commit is unconfirmed, that
residual is NOT the commit race — it is the reset itself. Cause unknown. Two
successive theories (wrong PICO_BOARD, then the commit race) each produced a real
improvement and neither was the whole story, so the next step is SWD and a backtrace
rather than a third theory.

The harness gained a distinction this run exposed: "no re-enumeration" has two
opposite causes. The firmware declining to reset also produces no new device
identity, but leaves the device ALIVE — reporting that as a hang would score the
safety mechanism as a failure. It now separates them by whether the device is still
on the bus.
