Retracts two wrong claims made earlier in this same document: that multiple
certificates break the GPG binding, and that GPG needs a token to itself. Both
were written from a symptom without finding the cause.

The cause is one missing guard in a released dependency. gnupg-pkcs11-scd skips
certificates whose algorithm it cannot parse via

    if (error == GPG_ERR_WRONG_PUBKEY_ALGO) { error = GPG_ERR_NO_ERROR; }

and in 0.11.0 that guard is in send_certificate_list() but ABSENT from
cmd_keyinfo(). Homebrew ships 0.11.0.

Confirmed by building master (0.11.1_master) and re-testing the SAME card with the
SAME EC certificate that wedged the released version:

    0.11.0          KEYINFO -> ERR 41, SOPS hangs
    0.11.1_master   KEYINFO -> OK,     SOPS decrypt rc=0, ERR 41 count 0

And the multi-purpose claim is now demonstrated rather than assumed: one card
holding RSA + P-256 + secp256k1, each with a certificate, served an SSH login to a
live sshd and a sops --decrypt in the same session, plus X.509 CA signing and
wallet signatures.

Build-from-master is therefore a REQUIREMENT, not a preference — 0.11.0 fails by
hanging, and a hung sops --decrypt in a deploy pipeline is worse than a clean error.

Also records two incidental findings: repeated certificate write/delete cycles
exhausted the object store (C_CreateObject -> CKR_GENERAL_ERROR until a wipe), and
deleting a certificate also removes the public key object, so the key still signs
but ssh-keygen -D can no longer enumerate it.
