Subject: [PATCH 15/27] fix(hsm): reset via SYSRESETREQ with flash quiesced,
 never the watchdog
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

SWD forensics on the ~7% re-provisioning hang (2026-08-02, Debug Probe on
the SWD port, xPack OpenOCD rp2350 target, hsm-cycle-test.sh):

- watchdog_reboot(): device left the USB bus, never re-enumerated.
  Forensics on the live hang: watchdog REASON.TIMER set (the watchdog DID
  fire), then both Cortex-M33 cores reading all-zero, XIP SSI all-zero,
  bootram diagnostics untouched — the post-reset boot never got as far as
  XIP setup.
- Reset TYPE ruled out: AIRCR.SYSRESETREQ hung with the same signature.
- Flash-busy-at-reset ruled out as the sole cause: holding the flash
  mutex across the reset hung identically (kept anyway — one less
  variable, and it is cheap).

So: 500ms grace for the APDU response, low_flash_quiesce(), then
AIRCR.SYSRESETREQ (both secure and non-secure bits, DSB barriers). No
watchdog fallback — it is a measured wedge vector. If the AIRCR write
does not reset, stay alive and report SW_EXEC_ERROR: an operator who can
retry beats a device that needs a datacenter visit. Watchdog scratch[0]
carries an attribution marker (0x1A17C0DE) so any future wedge can be
tied to this code path over SWD.

Submodule: core1 launch verification + dead-man's switch (see its
commit).
